-- D6 federal/state foundation continued: Governor + Lieutenant Governor for
-- all 50 states. Small hand-verified migration, not an ingester -- same
-- shape as migration 061's President/VP (no unified API covers statewide
-- executive offices, and this project's discipline is to never guess a
-- living person's office from training-data memory). Live-verified
-- 2026-08-12 against the National Governors Association roster (via
-- Wikipedia's "List of current United States governors," raw HTML table
-- extracted directly rather than trusted via an AI-summarized fetch --
-- this session already caught one such summary hallucinating a real fact
-- (Virginia's state-legislature election parity), so every large table
-- this session has been pulled and parsed as raw HTML instead) and, for
-- Lieutenant Governors, Wikipedia's matching "List of current United
-- States lieutenant governors" (sourced to The Green Papers). Spot-checked
-- against independently-known facts before trusting the bulk table:
-- Maryland (Wes Moore, D, Jan 2023) and New Jersey (Mikie Sherrill, D,
-- took office Jan 2026 after the Nov 2025 election) both matched.
--
-- All 50 states have a Governor; only 45 have a separately-modeled
-- Lieutenant Governor -- Arizona, Maine, New Hampshire, Oregon, and
-- Wyoming have no such office (confirmed by this exact 45-state list,
-- cross-checking the same 5-state gap identified earlier this session
-- from a different source). New Hampshire and Vermont are the only two
-- states with 2-year gubernatorial terms (and their Lt. Governor terms,
-- where applicable, follow the same length) -- every other state is 4
-- years. "Selection" (same ticket vs. separate election) doesn't change
-- how this schema models the office: like the Vice Presidency in
-- migration 061, every Lieutenant Governor here is still a standalone
-- is_elected=TRUE office, not folded into the Governor's bio text.
--
-- term_start is a disclosed approximation for incumbents who have won
-- consecutive terms without a gap: this table gives continuous-tenure-
-- since dates (when someone FIRST took office), not necessarily the date
-- of their most recent re-election -- e.g. Vermont's Governor first took
-- office in 2017 and has been re-elected since, but this migration
-- records 2017, not his most recent swearing-in. Same low-stakes
-- reasoning as db/ingest/openstates-legislature.mjs's own term_start
-- note: this field isn't displayed anywhere in the app currently
-- (confirmed via grep before either migration was written).



